{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "New Orleans, Opelousas and Great Western Railroad, Algiers - Brashear (Morgan City), Louisiana: the company's own eras 1854-1862 and 1868-1869, and the U.S. military operation of the road 1863-1865. Charles Morgan's era from 1870 (Morgan's Louisiana and Texas) is drawn by data/morgan_s_louisiana_and_texas_railroad_and_steamship_company.geojson, and from 1883 the Southern Pacific Sunset Route layer.",
  "source": "research/branches/new_orleans_opelousas_and_great_western_railroad.txt (researched 2026-11-12)",
  "pulled": "20261112",
  "ownership_split": "NOO&GW to 1862; U.S. military authorities (Department of the Gulf) 1 May/1 Jul 1862 - 31 Jan 1866, drawn 1863-1865 as usmrr-algiers-brashear-city on identical geometry; NOO&GW again 1 Feb 1866 - 31 Jul 1869 (drawn 1868-1869); Charles Morgan from 31 Jul 1869 (existing Morgan's layer, from 1870). New colours: NOO&GW #6B4F3A, U.S. Military Railroads #4B5320 - neither used by any other company in data/.",
  "omitted": "1866-1867 company operation (no passenger evidence read); 1862 under either operator after the takeover (west end lost to Confederates late May-Nov 1862; no travel account); sections west of Lafourche Crossing in 1855-56 (opened in undated stages). Graded-but-never-tracked Brashear - New Iberia - Opelousas roadbed (about 80 mi, sold 1872): never ran trains. Morgan's eras, branches and the Lafayette/Alexandria extensions are drawn by the Morgan's layer.",
  "not_yet_sourced": "Headways for 1854-1865 are estimates. The 1857-1862 frequency and the 1866-67 service are unread. The Aug 1863 'ready for travel' letter is known only from a secondary summary. Source conflict: U.S. takeover 1 May 1862 (ICC, Friedman) vs 1 Jul 1862 (company report). Sale to Morgan 31 Jul 1869 (ICC) vs 25 May 1869 (other source cited by Friedman).",
  "freight_only_check": "All drawn years have positive passenger evidence or bracket it; no freight-only segment drawn.",
  "horsecar_check": "None - steam from the first train in 1853."
 },
