    "speed_source": "Segment-level, 1868-69 only: the company's own ad (New Orleans Tribune, inserted 22 Nov 1868, read 28 Feb 1869) - train leaves Algiers depot 8 A.M., arrives Brashear City 12 M.; about 80 mi in 4 h = 32 km/h. 1857-62 not checked.",
    "geometry_source": "Copy of mlt-algiers-morgan-city (OSM-traced), whole length.",
    "geometry_quality": "Real-traced (via the Morgan's trace), no straight-line legs. Drawn 80.1 mi against 80.6 mi Algiers - Brashear/Morgan City (ICC 80.60). Brashear end at Morgan City on the east shore of Berwick Bay. Geometry copied programmatically from data/morgan_s_louisiana_and_texas_railroad_and_steamship_company.geojson feature mlt-algiers-morgan-city (same rails, successor operator), which is real-traced from OSM on the ORIGINAL west-bank alignment Algiers - Gretna - Westwego - Avondale, not the post-1935 Huey P. Long Bridge route. That trace's own caveats carry over: the Algiers end (about 3 mi through Algiers and Gretna) follows a razed grade and a river trail, and the Algiers terminal point is APPROXIMATE (the depot/ferry slip opposite Elysian Fields Ave was not located). LATER ALIGNMENT: drawn on the grade as OSM records it today; the 1874-75 track raising and any realignment since the 1850s were not checked.",
    "documented_mileage": 80.6,
    "traced_mileage": 80.1,
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1857,
      "end": 1862,
      "headway_min": 1440,
      "note": "Algiers - Brashear (renamed Morgan City 1876), completed 12 Apr 1857 (ICC: 'the last 7 miles being completed on April 12, 1857'). Brashear was the railhead for the next 23 years; trains met Morgan steamers to Galveston and Texas (twice weekly before the war, csa-railroads.com). The 1858 Dinsmore guide lists stations along it (Fowler, letter x); 29,000 passengers carried in 1860 (csa-railroads.com). Ends 1862: the U.S. military authorities of the Department of the Gulf took the road on 1 May 1862 (ICC, Friedman) or 1 Jul 1862 (company's own report) - a two-month source conflict, both in 1862. Jan-Apr 1862 was still company-run under the Confederate state. Headway 1440 is an ESTIMATE; no pre-war timetable frequency was read.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: completion date from the ICC valuation; 1858 stations from Fowler (Dinsmore 1858, letter x); 1860 passenger count and steamer connection from csa-railroads.com; end date from ICC/Friedman vs the company report (conflict recorded). Headway not sourced.",
