     {
      "start": 1868,
      "end": 1869,
      "headway_min": 2520,
      "note": "Company operation restored: the U.S. returned the road 1 Feb 1866 'in poor condition after repeated floods' (Friedman). Positive passenger evidence only from Nov 1868: a standing New Orleans Tribune ad (inserted 22 Nov 1868, read 28 Feb 1869), 'FOR TEXAS. INSIDE BERWICK'S BAY ROUTE, VIA NEW ORLEANS, OPELOUSAS AND GREAT WESTERN RAILROAD' - ferry from opposite Jackson Square Sun/Tue/Thu/Sat 7:30 a.m., train leaves Algiers depot 8 a.m., arrives Brashear City noon, connecting with Morgan Line steamships for Galveston; signed G. W. R. Bailey, Superintendent. 1866-67 is NOT drawn: no passenger evidence was read for those years (not proven absent either). Ends 1869: sold at foreclosure to Charles Morgan 31 Jul 1869 (ICC; another source 25 May 1869); the Morgan's layer starts at its first Morgan-name evidence in 1870. Headway 2520 = four through trains a week from the ad; whether locals ran daily is not established.",
