    "geometry_quality": "Real-traced (via the Morgan's trace), no straight-line legs. Drawn 80.1 mi against about 80 mi Algiers - Brashear City (ICC 80.60). Identical geometry to noogw-algiers-brashear (Rule 4 operator split). Geometry copied programmatically from data/morgan_s_louisiana_and_texas_railroad_and_steamship_company.geojson feature mlt-algiers-morgan-city (same rails, successor operator), which is real-traced from OSM on the ORIGINAL west-bank alignment Algiers - Gretna - Westwego - Avondale, not the post-1935 Huey P. Long Bridge route. That trace's own caveats carry over: the Algiers end (about 3 mi through Algiers and Gretna) follows a razed grade and a river trail, and the Algiers terminal point is APPROXIMATE (the depot/ferry slip opposite Elysian Fields Ave was not located). LATER ALIGNMENT: drawn on the grade as OSM records it today; the 1874-75 track raising and any realignment since the 1850s were not checked.",
    "documented_mileage": 80.6,
    "traced_mileage": 80.1,
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1863,
      "end": 1865,
      "headway_min": 1440,
      "note": "The New Orleans, Opelousas and Great Western run by the U.S. military authorities of the Department of the Gulf from 1 May / 1 Jul 1862 to 31 Jan 1866 (ICC; company report); Friedman: 'U.S. Military Railroads took over the line with 12 locomotives, 12 passenger cars'. Drawn 1863-1865 only: the west end was retaken by Confederates late May-Nov 1862, Confederates briefly held Brashear City in June 1863, and the first passenger-travel account is a correspondent's letter of 14 Aug 1863 that the line was 'again ready for travel' (secondary report, not read in full). 4 Oct 1863 a regiment 'took the cars for Brashear City' (Ann Arbor Register 11 Aug 1887, reminiscence) - a troop train. Civilian fare-paying service is confirmed by the New Orleans Tribune of 24 Nov 1865 on 'the Opelousas Railroad, now in charge of the Federal authorities'. If troop trains are not counted as passenger service, this would shrink to 1865 alone. Headway 1440 is an ESTIMATE.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: operating dates from the ICC valuation and the company report of 31 Dec 1866; passenger evidence New Orleans Tribune 24 Nov 1865 p3 and Ann Arbor Register 11 Aug 1887; the Aug 1863 letter is known only at second hand.",
